::: {.doc-subject}

Add a path graph (linear chain) to the current graph.
:::

::: {.doc-usage-source}
Usage

[Source](https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase/blob/main/python/edgecase/graph.py#L7724-L7844){target="_blank" rel="noopener"}
:::

::: {.doc-signature .doc-function}
```pythonsignature
Graph.add_path_graph(
    n,
    type=None,
    label=True,
    rel=None,
)
```
:::

::: {.doc-text}

![Path graph](../assets/graphs/add-path-graph.light.svg){.lightbox fig-alt="Three path graphs side by side: two nodes joined by one edge for n=2, a chain of three nodes for n=3, and a chain of four nodes for n=4. Each is a single line in which the two end nodes have one neighbor and every inner node has two." width="100%"}

Creates a sequence of `n` nodes connected linearly: `1 -> 2 -> 3 -> ... -> n`. End nodes
have degree `1`, internal nodes have degree 2.

A path graph (often written P_n) has `n` nodes and `n - 1` edges, is connected, and
contains no cycles, so it is the simplest possible tree. Its diameter is `n - 1`, the
largest of any connected graph with `n` nodes. That makes it a handy test case and
building block:

- checking algorithms against known answers (shortest paths, diameter, centrality)
- modelling linear structures like pipelines, supply chains, or sequences of events
- adding a "tail" or chain onto another structure

Each node is labelled with its own node ID as a string: in an empty graph they are `"1"` to
`"n"`, and if the graph already has 3 nodes they are `"4"` to `"n + 3"`. In a directed graph
the edges point from each node to the next. The path is added as a new, separate component;
it isn't connected to any existing nodes. The graph is modified in place, and the whole
construction is recorded as a single step in the step log (see `steps`).
:::

## Parameters {.doc-parameters}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code>[n]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [int]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   Number of nodes in the path. Must be at least `1` (a single node with no edges); the
    path will have `n - 1` edges.

<code>[type]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [Optional[str]]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>

:   Optional node type/category assigned to all new nodes (stored in the `type` column of
    the node DataFrame). Default is `None`, which leaves the type unset.

<code>[label]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [bool]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [[True]{.va}]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>

:   If `True` (default), label each node with its ID as described above, so labels match
    IDs even after nodes have been deleted. If one of those labels is already used in the
    graph, a `ValueError` is raised before anything is added. If
    `False`, the nodes are left unlabelled.

<code>[rel]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [Optional[str]]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>

:   Optional relationship label assigned to all edges of the path (stored in the `rel`
    column of the edge DataFrame). Default is `None`.
:::

## Returns {.doc-returns}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code> [Graph]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   Self for method chaining.
:::

## Raises {.doc-raises}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code> [ValueError]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   If `n=` is less than `1`.
:::

## See Also {.doc-see-also}

[](`~add_cycle_graph`) 

:   Add a cycle graph.

[](`~add_star_graph`) 

:   Add a star graph.

[](`~diameter`) 

:   Compute the longest shortest-path distance.

## Examples {.doc-examples}

A path graph joins `n` nodes end to end in a line, so it has one edge fewer than it has
nodes. The two ends have degree `1`, every inner node has degree `2`, and the ends are as
far apart as any two of the nodes can be:

::: {#ef2d9893 .cell execution_count=1}
``` {.python .cell-code}
import edgecase as ec

graph = ec.Graph().add_path_graph(n=5)

degrees = graph.get_node_df()["degree"].to_list()

graph.count_edges(), degrees, graph.diameter()
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}
```
(4, [1, 2, 2, 2, 1], 4)
```
:::
:::


In a directed graph each edge points to the next node. `type=` and `rel=` tag the nodes
and edges, which suits a linear process such as a train line:

::: {#6d309975 .cell execution_count=2}
``` {.python .cell-code}
line = ec.Graph(directed=True)
line.add_path_graph(n=4, type="stop", rel="next")

line.get_edges_str_repr(use_labels=True)
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=2}
```
['1->2', '2->3', '3->4']
```
:::
:::


### One path or two

Calling it again adds a second path, not joined to the first, whose labels continue the
numbering. A path of six on the left, and two paths of three on the right:

::: {layout-ncol="2"}

::: {#4d71b4d6 .cell execution_count=3}
``` {.python .cell-code}
ec.Graph().add_path_graph(n=6).plot(
    label_by="label", width=320, height=320, legend=False, seed=1
)
```

